-6 rounded-lg bg-cadet-gray bg-opacity-5"><p class=3D"text-white text-base =
md:text-lg word-break whitespace-pre-line">&lt;!-- patchstack-triage --&gt;=
<br>Still vulnerable in version 2.2.2:<br>- The whole attribute array, incl=
uding the unsanitized `button.text`, is written into `data-attributes` thro=
ugh `esc_attr( wp_json_encode() )`; `esc_attr` does not double-encode, so a=
 value saved as `&amp;lt;svg&amp;gt;=E2=80=A6` (which gets past the kses fi=
lter WordPress applies to block attributes) reaches the browser's attribute=
 parser as `&amp;lt;` and comes out as a real `&lt;svg&gt;` string after JS=
ON.parse. build/render.php:70. Fix: Sanitize `$attributes['button']['text']=
` with `wp_kses_post()` (applied to the decoded value) as is already done f=
or `sliders[].title`/`desc`, or better, stop using `esc_attr` as the only d=
efence for HTML-bearing values and sanitize every attribute that the fronte=
nd renders as HTML.<br>- The post-slide component sets `g =3D O(s, i, u?.te=
xt)`, which returns the attacker's `button.text` unchanged when no ACF over=
ride is set, and passes it to the anchor component as `dangerouslySetInnerH=
TML:{__html:g}`, so the string is parsed as HTML and an `&lt;svg&gt;&lt;ani=
mate onbegin=3D=E2=80=A6&gt;` payload runs JavaScript as soon as the page l=
oads. build/view.js:1. Fix: Render the button label as text (pass it as `ch=
ildren`) or run it through a sanitizer such as DOMPurify before using `dang=
erouslySetInnerHTML`.<br><br>Attacker role: Contributor.<br>Impact: Stored =
XSS: JavaScript a Contributor saves in the block runs in the browser of any=
one who views the post, including administrators who preview it, which allo=
ws session-riding actions such as creating admin accounts.<br><br>Hardening=
 suggestions (not blockers):<br>- `O()`/`L()` in view.js also feeds ACF fie=
ld values (`buttonText`, `title`) into `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, so any AC=
F value an author can edit is a second route to the same sink. build/view.j=
s:1. Fix: Sanitize ACF-sourced values server-side in `Posts::arrangedPosts(=
)` or on the client before inserting them as HTML.<br>- In the `blocks` sou=
rce type, the whole inner block content is base64-encoded into `data-attrib=
utes-b64` and put back into the page with innerHTML, without going through =
the core block render filters. build/render.php:11. Fix: Run `$content` thr=
ough `wp_kses_post()` (after the shortcodes are expanded) before embedding =
it, unless the author has `unfiltered_html`.<br>&lt;!-- /patchstack-triage =
--&gt;</p><p class=3D"text-cadet-gray text-sm mt-4"> Last updated: 19 Oct 2=
026 at 15:57</p></div></div></div><div class=3D"flex justify-start items-ba=
seline gap-4"><h1 class=3D"text-white text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold">De=
scription</h1><p class=3D"text-x text-yellow"> CVSS 6.5</p></div><p class=
=3D"text-white text-base md:text-lg word-break">A Contributor Stored Cross-=
Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability exists in the bSlider plugin &lt;=3D 2.2=
.2 due to insufficient sanitization of Gutenberg block attributes rendered =
on the frontend. While the plugin sanitizes `sliders[].title` and `sliders[=
].desc` using `wp_kses_post()` in `build/render.php`, it leaves the button =
configuration attribute (`button.text`) uninspected and unescaped. When ren=
dering post sliders on the frontend, `build/view.js` injects the button tex=
t directly into the DOM via React's `dangerouslySetInnerHTML: { __html: g }=
` without any sanitization. An attacker with the Contributor role can craft=
 a post containing a `bsb/slider` block with a malicious `&lt;svg&gt;` tag =
in the `button.text` attribute, executing arbitrary JavaScript in any visit=
or's or administrator's browser upon page load.<br>The bSlider plugin provi=
des the `bsb/slider` Gutenberg block for displaying carousels and sliders o=
f posts or images. In `build/render.php`, although the developer implemente=
d sanitization for slide titles and descriptions via `wp_kses_post()`, the =
`button` attribute (`button.text`) is passed unescaped directly into the wr=
apper element's `data-attributes`:<br><br>```<br>// build/render.php lines =
46-73<br>foreach ( ( isset( $attributes['sliders'] ) ? $attributes['sliders=
'] : [] ) as $index =3D&gt; $slider ) {<br>    $sliders[] =3D $slider;<br> =
   $sliders[ $index ]['title'] =3D isset( $slider['title'] ) ? wp_kses_post=
( $slider['title'] ) : '';<br>    $sliders[ $index ]['desc'] =3D isset( $sl=
ider['desc'] ) ? wp_kses_post( $slider['desc'] ) : '';<br>}<br>$attributes[=
'sliders'] =3D $sliders;<br>// ...<br>&lt;div id=3D'bsbCarousel-...' data-a=
ttributes=3D'&lt;?php echo esc_attr( wp_json_encode( $attributes ) ); ?&gt;=
'&gt;<br>```<br><br>On the frontend, `build/view.js` parses `data-attribute=
s` on `DOMContentLoaded` and renders the slider items. In component `An` (r=
endering post slides):<br>```<br>g =3D !1 !=3D=3D u?.isVisible ? O(s, i, u?=
.text) : ""; // u is button, u.text is the button label<br>// ...<br>(0, e.=
createElement)(y, {<br>    href: E,<br>    linkTarget: p?.linkTarget,<br>  =
  isBackEnd: r,<br>    isSelected: o,<br>    dangerouslySetInnerHTML: { __h=
tml: g } // Vulnerable Sink without sanitization<br>})<br>```</p><div class=
